# Point the app at your machine's backend.
# iOS simulator: http://localhost:3000
# Android emulator: http://10.0.2.2:3000
# Physical device + unrestricted LAN (rare on eduroam): http://<your-lan-ip>:3000 (backend console).
# If Safari on the phone cannot open /health, use a tunnel instead (backend running first):
#   cd backend && npm run tunnel
# Copy the https://....trycloudflare.com URL (no trailing slash) here, restart Expo --clear.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# `npm run start:tunnel` uses Expo's **bundled ngrok 2.x agent** (`@expo/ngrok-bin`), not your Homebrew
# ngrok 3.x. Many ngrok accounts now expect agent v3+, so tunnel mode often fails with `remote gone away`
# no matter what you put in `.env`. On campus Wi‑Fi use **`npm run start:cloudflare`** (see README).
#
# Eduroam / client isolation: one command starts Cloudflare → :8081 and Expo with EXPO_PACKAGER_PROXY_URL.
# Uses cloudflared **HTTP/2 (TCP)** by default (QUIC/UDP is often blocked on campus). Optional: EDUROAM_EXPO_VERBOSE=1
#   npm run start:cloudflare
# (See https://github.com/expo/expo/issues/43335 )
